// Generated by website/sync-docs.mjs from docs/. Do not edit by hand.
window.TSGPU_DOCS = {
  "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md": "# Architecture and milestones\r\n\r\n## Source pipeline\r\n\r\nThe `renderer` setting selects the host:\r\n\r\n- `rendrix` (default): parse the supported HTML subset into a keyed tree, expand static components, parse CSS with PostCSS, and render through the Rendrix engine (winit + wgpu + taffy + cosmic-text). Actions run in embedded QuickJS. **Rendrix is in early beta**; feature coverage is limited, and state-driven re-render, hover/active/focus, scrolling, images, text editing, accessibility, and animations are on the Rendrix M1–M3 ladder (see [RENDERER_TSGPU.md](RENDERER_TSGPU.md) and [REWRITE_RENDRIX.md](REWRITE_RENDRIX.md)).\r\n- `native`: the deprecated GPUI host. Accepted with a deprecation warning until the Rendrix M1 gate removes it.\r\n- `webview`: a hard error. The WebView renderer was removed in the rewrite; see below.\r\n\r\nBoth paths bundle project-local TypeScript backend modules and emit a UTF-8 JSON `.tgp` bundle. On Windows and Linux release packaging appends `[bundle][u64 LE length][TSGPU001]` to the matching prebuilt host executable. macOS app bundles store the document in `Contents/Resources/tsgpu.tgp`. Packaged apps do not need Node or Bun at runtime.\r\n\r\n## Removed: WebView renderer\r\n\r\nThe WebView renderer (Wry, WebView2/WKWebView/WebKitGTK) and all of its paths were deleted in the rewrite: the webview host binary and `webview-host` feature, the `gtk`/`tao`/`wry` dependencies, the webview compiler/packaging paths, the page bridge and custom protocol, CDP-based e2e, and the WebKitGTK Linux CI dependencies. The pre-rewrite implementation is preserved at branch `backup/webview-era` (commit `0fc860b`). Historical summaries of the removed protocol/bridge behavior live in [SUPPORTED_SYNTAX.md](SUPPORTED_SYNTAX.md) and [SAFETY.md](SAFETY.md).\r\n\r\n[`platform.rs`](../native/src/platform.rs) holds the operations the hosts share: clipboard, native dialogs, power blockers, external links and the HTTPS transport. Each is implemented once, so `api.native` answers the same way on the Rendrix and GPUI hosts.\r\n\r\n`tsgpu import electron <dir> [--out DIR] [--build] [--force]` imports an Electron project using [`import-electron.mjs`](../packages/cli/src/import-electron.mjs). The importer produces a review report; compatibility is limited to implemented shims and must be reviewed for each app. `--build` runs the source project's build script. Every Electron export is classified as implemented, partial or unsupported in the coverage ledger ([`coverage.mjs`](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/coverage.mjs)). A test checks the ledger against an extracted snapshot of Electron 33's public API, and each unsupported shim throws an error that points to the import report.\r\n\r\nThe Electron shims speak the host call protocol: `host(operation, args)` with named arguments and normalized results. In the renderer backend the shim has no bridge object, so [`host.ts`](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/runtime/host.ts) routes each operation to `api.native` and reshapes its result. An operation with no route rejects and names the renderer that provides it. The routes are tested in [`electron-native.test.mjs`](../packages/cli/test/electron-native.test.mjs). (The webview-era page bridge that also spoke this protocol was removed with the WebView renderer.)\r\n\r\n## Native host modules\r\n\r\nThe deprecated GPUI host is organized under `native/src/view/`: `mod.rs` owns app state and window lifecycle, `rendering.rs` builds the element tree, `inputs.rs` handles text editing and IME, `overlays.rs` draws menus and development panels, `layout.rs` resolves styles and grid tracks, and `bindings.rs` handles state paths, text interpolation, shortcuts, and form validation. The remaining host concerns stay in focused sibling modules such as `package.rs`, `runtime.rs`, `network.rs`, and `menus.rs`. The Rendrix engine lives in `native/src/gpu/` (that directory is extracted into an independent open-source `rendrix` crate in Phase B).\r\n\r\n## Build and development\r\n\r\nBuild a host when Rust host code changes. Use `tsgpu host build --renderer rendrix` for the Rendrix engine or `tsgpu host build --renderer native` for the deprecated GPUI host. HTML, CSS, TypeScript, and asset changes only rebuild the `.tgp` bundle. Hosts are target-specific: Windows produces PE, Linux ELF, and macOS Mach-O. `tsgpu build` packages the matching host and creates a macOS `.app` directory bundle; it does not sign or notarize that bundle. `--target` and `tsgpu all` package the same bundle for other supported targets, each with its own verified prebuilt host.\r\n\r\n`tsgpu dev` watches the configured source files and rewrites the bundle. Host processes reload the bundle in the existing window. The GPUI host preserves backend state across reloads by merging user-changed values into the new script; the Rendrix host's reload behavior is part of the early beta and follows the same merge model as it matures. A failed build is shown in the GPUI host's in-window panel while the last successful document keeps running. The browser preview (`tsgpu preview`) preserves state on reload.\r\n\r\n## Native controls and capabilities\r\n\r\nThe host implements buttons, text inputs, textareas, checkboxes, selects, form submission, numeric validation, keyboard shortcuts, Tab focus traversal, repeated state arrays, scrolling, CSS grid layout, and packaged images. Single-line `<input>` fields register with GPUI's text input handler, so platform text input and IME composition go through the OS text services. They draw a blinking caret and selection highlight, support mouse click and drag selection, Shift+arrow, Home/End, word-wise navigation and deletion, Ctrl/Cmd+A/C/X/V/Z, Shift+Ctrl/Cmd+Z, Ctrl/Cmd+Y, and Backspace/Delete. Undo history is bounded and kept per field. Caret and selection are measured with the element's inherited text style. Double-click selects a word. Multi-line `<textarea>` fields share the same path: each logical line is shaped to the field width in GPUI's text system, and paint draws the selection, text, and caret from that layout, so positions match what is drawn. The field scrolls with the wheel and keeps the caret in view.\r\n\r\nApplication menus are declared in the document and installed through GPUI's application menu API. Menu actions and shortcuts dispatch through the backend action path. Context menus render as host popups; disabled entries work there. Native application menu items cannot currently be disabled, and checked menu items are not supported. Elements with `data-ondrop` receive user-dropped local file paths and names through GPUI's `ExternalPaths`; the host does not read file contents.\r\n\r\nLists marked `data-virtual` become GPUI uniform lists, so only the visible rows are built. A unified title bar is an app-drawn bar with `appears_transparent`. Its drag area and window buttons are reported to Windows as caption and caption-button hit regions, so no control can sit inside a drag area. An optional blurred backdrop uses GPUI's `Blurred` background, which Windows applies only on some systems. Selects open a positioned native popup with the current option marked; Up/Down keys change the value directly.\r\n\r\nThe development overlay is host UI drawn above the document. `F12` or `Ctrl+Shift+I` toggles it, and the inspector mode disables app controls while a click selects the deepest element and records its attributes, computed style, and state styles. A stale element selection is cleared on reload.\r\n\r\nWith `\"persist\": true`, a background task serializes backend state every 400 ms when it changes, caps it at 256 KiB, and writes it through a temporary file plus rename. Startup merges the saved values with the script's current defaults through the same compatibility rules as hot reload.\r\n\r\nThe GPUI 0.2.2 API in this project does not expose an accessibility tree interface. HTML `aria-*` and `role` metadata is preserved, but screen-reader semantics are not wired to the native OS APIs yet. The Rendrix roadmap adds an accesskit tree at M3; see [ACCESSIBILITY_PLAN.md](ACCESSIBILITY_PLAN.md).\r\n\r\nClipboard actions run only after a button click. File reads and writes require the native file picker/save dialog, operate on the user-selected path, and are limited to 1 MiB. Network actions are user-initiated HTTPS requests to exact hosts listed in `permissions.network`; GET and POST are supported, callers can send a state value as JSON or text, redirects are disabled, requests time out after 15 seconds, and request and response bodies are limited to 1 MiB. Backend JavaScript does not receive a general filesystem or `fetch` API.\r\n\r\n## Backend design\r\n\r\nThe backend runs in one embedded QuickJS context with in-memory application state. Actions receive `get`, `set`, and `update`, plus a read-only event object. Actions can await the native host bridge, whose operations run asynchronously. Arbitrary external JavaScript APIs are not available in the native backend.\r\n\r\nQuickJS memory and execution time are limited as described in the safety documentation; the runtime is not a malicious-code sandbox. Only load backend code you trust.\r\n\r\n## Versioned binary format\r\n\r\nOn Windows and Linux a packaged application consists of the native executable followed by JSON bytes, an 8-byte little-endian payload length, and `TSGPU001`. The Windows host also locates this trailer before an Authenticode certificate table. macOS uses a bundle resource. The host limits embedded payload reads to 16 MiB. The bundle carries an optional SHA-256 integrity field over its backend and root; the host verifies it on load when present and rejects tampered bundles. The outer container itself is still not encrypted.\r\n\r\n## Remaining roadmap\r\n\r\n1. Run platform-level interaction and packaging checks on Windows, macOS, and Linux. Windows host build and interaction have also been exercised by hand; check workflow results separately.\r\n2. Connect role, label, and value metadata to a native accessibility tree when the supported GPUI API provides it.\r\n3. Improve the Rendrix layout model: grid item placement, stacking contexts beyond sibling paint order, and more CSS layout properties. Fixed, automatic, and unequal `fr` column tracks now preserve their proportions through flex-based layout. CSS transitions, animations, and element transforms need the animation/drawing primitives on the Rendrix M3 ladder; no in-tree engine supports them today.\r\n4. Add a nonblocking async backend bridge with cancellation and explicit per-operation permission grants.\r\n5. Add a native code editor or terminal component for apps that need one, and executable signing/update support.\r\n\r\n## Alternatives considered\r\n\r\n- Rewriting the renderer in TypeScript would abandon the native engine and require a complete GUI toolkit.\r\n- Transpiling arbitrary TypeScript to Rust is not practical with broad JavaScript semantics.\r\n- Keeping the WebView renderer as a compatibility path was rejected in the rewrite decision: two rendering stacks double the maintenance surface, and the Rendrix engine is the product's direction ([REWRITE_RENDRIX.md](REWRITE_RENDRIX.md)).\r\n- Recompiling Rust for every developer edit is much slower than reusing one host executable.\r\n\r\nThe design keeps rendering native and iteration fast, with a documented HTML subset and explicit host capabilities. Rendrix replaces the GPUI renderer as the sole engine; the GPUI host stays deprecated in the tree until the Rendrix M1 gate.\r\n",
  "docs/SUPPORTED_SYNTAX.md": "# Supported HTML, CSS, and TypeScript\r\n\r\nThe TS-GPU compiler turns a documented subset of HTML, CSS, and TypeScript into a `.tgp` bundle. One rendering engine reads it: **Rendrix**, the TS-GPU engine.\r\n\r\n> **Rendrix is in early beta.** Feature coverage is limited — state-driven re-render, hover/active/focus states, scrolling, images, text editing, accessibility, and animations are still on the [Rendrix roadmap](RENDERER_TSGPU.md). It is a long way from stable. If you relied on the removed WebView renderer, consult the pre-rewrite implementation at branch `backup/webview-era` (tag `webview-era-checkpoint`, commit `0fc860b`).\r\n\r\n## Renderer selection\r\n\r\nSet the project renderer in `tsgpu.config.json`:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"renderer\": \"rendrix\"\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`renderer` accepts `rendrix` (the default; omitting the key selects it too) or `native`, which selects the deprecated GPUI host and prints a deprecation warning until the Rendrix M1 gate removes it. `webview` is a hard error: the WebView renderer was removed in the rewrite. `tsgpu dev` and `tsgpu build` select the matching host automatically.\r\n\r\n### Removed: WebView renderer\r\n\r\nThe WebView renderer (Wry on WebView2, WKWebView, and WebKitGTK) was deleted with the rewrite. Behavior that only that renderer had is preserved here as history; the full pre-rewrite text of this document is on branch `backup/webview-era`:\r\n\r\n- Multi-page bundles via `webviewPages` (project-relative paths), an app-local custom URL served through a `tsgpu` custom protocol with 404 on missing assets ([`webview_main.rs`](../native/src/webview_main.rs)), and a 256 MiB default `maxBundleSize`.\r\n- The injected page bridge `window.tsgpu.invoke` and `window.tsgpu.on` ([`webview_bridge.rs`](../native/src/webview_bridge.rs)); window operations executed on the UI thread, other host requests ran on one worker thread, and IPC send and invoke were dispatched in the page and required IPC permission.\r\n- Backend state surviving a `dev` reload in `sessionStorage`, or across app restarts in `localStorage` with `\"persist\": true`.\r\n- A generated page content security policy: build nonces for script elements, `unsafe-eval` for the backend bootstrap, inline styles allowed, `connect-src` limited to the page origin and the configured HTTPS network hosts under browser CORS rules. See [SAFETY.md](SAFETY.md).\r\n\r\nUse `tsgpu import electron <dir> [--out DIR] [--build] [--force]` to import an Electron project, or pass the project directory to `tsgpu run`, `tsgpu build` or a target shortcut, which converts it into a new temporary directory first. `dev`, `compile` and `preview` expect a TS-GPU project. The source project is not modified; the importer writes `TSGPU_IMPORT_REPORT.md` and prints its path. Review the report: each Electron API the app uses is checked against the [coverage ledger](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/coverage.mjs), and partial or unsupported APIs are listed. `--build` runs the source project's build script, and a conversion with no built renderer runs it as well ([`import-electron.mjs`](../packages/cli/src/import-electron.mjs)).\r\n\r\nThe Electron `BrowserWindow` shim provides `show()`, `hide()`, `isVisible()`, `setFullScreen()`/`isFullScreen()`/`setFullScreenable()`, `setBounds()`/`setPosition()`/`setSize()`/`getBounds()`/`getSize()`/`getPosition()`, `isMaximized()`/`isMinimized()`, and `focus()`/`blur()` for easy migration. `ready-to-show` is emitted after creation and after each `loadFile()`/`loadURL()`; `show()`/`hide()` emit `show`/`hide` and `setFullScreen()` emits `enter-full-screen`/`leave-full-screen` on additional (non-primary) windows, which also route via `window.show`/`window.hide`/`window.setFullScreen` and track `isVisible()`/`isFullScreen()` from the last requested state. `setBounds()`/`setPosition()`/`setSize()` track bounds locally, emit `will-move`/`will-resize` stubs before `move`/`resize`, and route via `window.setBounds`/`window.setPosition`/`window.setSize` on additional windows (the primary window is tracked locally only, without moving native chrome). `focus()`/`blur()` update the local focused window and route via `window.focus`/`window.blur` on additional windows; `isMaximized()`/`isMinimized()` report local state updated by `minimize()`/`maximize()`/`restore()` and host `focus`/`blur`/`move`/`resize` events, and `getFocusedWindow()` returns the locally focused window. The primary window's `show()`/`hide()` resolve without a host call and `setFullScreen()` throws `BrowserWindow fullscreen not supported on this renderer`. See the coverage ledger for the full `BrowserWindow` note, including the stub nature of `will-move`/`will-resize` (no geometry is carried) and the remaining parent/modal, preload, and IPC limits.\r\n\r\nThe Electron `session` shim provides `session.defaultSession`, `session.fromPartition(partition)` and `fromPath(path)`. Cookie control is available as `defaultSession.cookies.get/set/remove` (with `getCookies`/`setCookies`/`clearCookies` aliases). Calls resolve via `host('session.cookies')` when the host implements it; otherwise cookies are held in an in-memory `Map` per partition, and `persist:` partitions also mirror non-session cookies to a JSON file under `userData` so they survive restarts. `clearStorageData`/`clearData` filter storage types and fall back to cookies plus current-page `localStorage`; permission request/check handlers are stored and invocable through `requestPermission`/`checkPermission`; `setUserAgent` applies to the host or the current page; `webRequest` records listeners without interception; and `downloadURL`/`will-download` plus `createInterruptedDownload` provide `DownloadItem` lifecycle stubs. The importer maps `new BrowserWindow({ webPreferences: { partition } })`, `session.defaultSession`, `session.getSession`, or any `cookies`/`getCookies`/`setCookies`/`clearCookies` usage to `\"persist\": true` so page state (via `localStorage`) and cookies survive restarts. Proxy, cache, extension and network-emulation controls remain host-dependent stubs. See the coverage ledger and [`PROTOCOL_SESSION.md`](PROTOCOL_SESSION.md) for the full notes.\r\n\r\nThe Electron `protocol` shim handles in-page custom schemes (`app`, `file`, and allowlisted custom schemes). `protocol.handle(scheme, handler)` and `protocol.registerFileProtocol(scheme, handler?)` record a handler, and `BrowserWindow.loadURL('app://...')` / `loadURL('file://...')` rewrite the URL onto the host's packaged `tsgpu://localhost/` space, so `app://localhost/index.html` loads the packaged document (`webviewPages`, the multi-page list, was a webview-era feature and is historical). Handlers may return paths, `{data, mimeType}` buffers, response-like objects, or `{url, method, headers, uploadData}` remote proxies that run through the permission-checked HTTPS transport; binary and streaming bodies (ReadableStream/async iterables) are normalized. `protocol.registerSchemesAsPrivileged` validates and records standard/secure/CORS/fetch/CSP/service-worker/stream/code-cache flags, with `secure` and `corsEnabled` applied during dispatch; browser-level CORS, service-worker and CSP behaviour was the removed WebView renderer's, and those flags are historical for the in-tree engines. `protocol.unhandle`, `isProtocolHandled`/`isProtocolRegistered`/`isProtocolIntercepted`, and `unregisterProtocol` manage the registry. Handlers that compute paths dynamically still run through `dispatchRequest`; the importer allowlists literal schemes into `protocolSchemes` in `tsgpu.config.json` and warns when a scheme is dynamic. Remote schemes (`http`, `https`, `ws`, `wss`) cannot be used with `handle`/`register*Protocol`: HTTPS uses the permission-checked `permissions.network` transport, and `registerHttpProtocol` for a remote scheme throws. `intercept*Protocol` does accept remote schemes for explicit `dispatchRequest` calls, but the removed WebView renderer's page network was not intercepted. See [`PROTOCOL_SESSION.md`](PROTOCOL_SESSION.md) for examples and the remaining limits.\r\n\r\nIt supports `data-bind`, `{{state.path}}` text interpolation, `data-onclick`, `data-oninput`, forms with `data-submit`, `data-fetch`, file selection/read/save, and clipboard bindings. The removed WebView renderer accepted a smaller binding subset (no `data-repeat`, `data-virtual`, compile-time components, native window controls, or per-element CSS warnings) and kept backend state in `sessionStorage` (or `localStorage` with `\"persist\": true`). That behavior is historical.\r\n\r\n(Historical, WebView renderer:) the host installed `window.tsgpu.invoke(op, args)` and `window.tsgpu.on(channel, handler)` before page scripts, the backend used `__tsgpu_host` and `__tsgpu_emit`, and IPC was routed in the page when `permissions.ipc` was enabled. Window operations required `permissions.window`; filesystem roots and processes required their respective grants. Windows provided dialog and clipboard operations via Windows Forms; these operations on other platforms reject as unsupported. `notifications.show` is supported on the native renderer (desktop toast, or a notification on macOS and Linux). On the removed WebView renderer the host additionally showed an OS toast via notify-rust (WinRT toast on Windows, Notification Center on macOS, D-Bus/Notifications on Linux) when `permissions.notifications` was set, with `Notification.show()` in the Electron shim logging the failure, recording a compatibility warning, and falling back to an in-page toast; that fallback never threw, so `show()` kept its void, non-throwing contract. That path is historical.\r\n\r\nThe remaining HTML, CSS, and backend reference describes the Rendrix engine and the deprecated GPUI renderer unless a section says otherwise.\r\n\r\n## HTML\r\n\r\nSupported elements: `html`, `body`, `main`, `header`, `footer`, `nav`, `aside`, `section`, `article`, `div`, `h1` through `h6`, `p`, `span`, `strong`, `small`, `blockquote`, `pre`, `code`, `figure`, `figcaption`, `details`, `summary`, `table`, `caption`, `thead`, `tbody`, `tfoot`, `tr`, `th`, `td`, `button`, `label`, `input`, `textarea`, `select`, `option`, `form`, `ul`, `ol`, `li`, `img`, `menu`, `menuitem`, `hr`, `template`, `use`, and `slot`.\r\n\r\nSemantic and table elements currently render through the native container layout; use CSS flex or grid to control their layout. The renderer does not yet provide browser table algorithms, disclosure behavior, or heading defaults.\r\n\r\nCommon attributes include `id`, `class`, `title`, `aria-label`, `role`, `tabindex`, `name`, and `value`. Controls also support `type`, `placeholder`, `checked`, `selected`, `disabled`, `required`, `min`, `max`, and `step`.\r\n\r\n- `data-bind=\"stateKey\"` displays backend state. Text supports `{{stateKey}}` interpolation. Dotted paths work for nested objects and repeated items.\r\n- `data-onclick=\"actionName\"` runs a button action. `data-oninput=\"actionName\"` runs when an input, textarea, or select value changes. Actions receive an event object with `type` plus fields such as `value`, `checked`, `name`, and `submit`.\r\n- `<form data-submit=\"save\">` submits through a `button type=\"submit\"`. Required fields and numeric `min`, `max`, and `step` constraints are checked before dispatch.\r\n- Single-line text inputs support typing through the platform text input (including IME composition), a blinking caret, selection by mouse or Shift+arrow, Home/End, word-wise movement and deletion, Ctrl/Cmd+A/C/X/V/Z, Shift+Ctrl/Cmd+Z and Ctrl/Cmd+Y, Tab focus traversal, and password masking. Undo history is per field, bounded, and reset on app reload. Textareas use the same native text path: multi-line caret and selection, IME, clipboard, wrapping to the field width, scrolling by wheel and by caret, Enter for a new line, Up/Down by visual row, Home/End by line, Ctrl/Cmd+Home/End for the whole text, and double-click to select a word. The default height is about three lines unless the element sets `height`.\r\n- A select opens a native dropdown popup on click. Up/Down keys also change the value, and Escape closes the popup.\r\n- `data-repeat=\"items as item\"` renders one node for each array element in backend state. Use `{{item.name}}` for item values. Nested repeats and state-bound inputs use the array index in their state path. Add `data-virtual=\"36\"` (a row height in pixels, from 1 to 1000) to build only the visible rows of a scrolling list. Every row must be exactly that height, and rows fill the list's width unless they set one. Without `data-virtual`, every row is built.\r\n- `<template data-component=\"Card\">...</template>` and `<use component=\"Card\" data-prop-title=\"Hello\"/>` expand static reusable components at compile time. A component can read string props with `{{props.title}}`. Recursive components and duplicate expanded IDs are rejected.\r\n\r\n- Slots: `<slot></slot>` inside a template receives the children written between `<use>` tags. `<slot name=\"footer\">Default</slot>` receives children marked `slot=\"footer\"`. Text inside a slot is its fallback, used when the caller gives nothing. Content with no matching slot is an error. `<use .../>` with no children is self-closed, and `<use ...>...</use>` takes slot content.\r\n- Attributes written on `<use>`, such as `class`, `data-bind`, `data-oninput`, `data-onclick`, `placeholder`, or `title`, go onto the component's first element. `class` names are merged. The element is checked again, so `data-onclick` on a component whose first element is not a `<button>` is an error.\r\n- Slot content keeps the props of the place where it is written. `{{props.x}}` in content written at the top level is empty, and a nested component's own `{{props.*}}` text shows that component's props.\r\n- Built-in library: `Sidebar`, `Toolbar` (slots `start` and `end`; `title` is the fallback text), `SegmentedControl`, `SearchField` (an `<input type=\"search\">`, so `data-bind` and `data-oninput` go on the `<use>`), `ListRow` (`title`, with a default slot for detail text), `Table`, `TableHeader`, `TableRow`, `TableHeaderCell`, `TableCell`, `CodeEditor` (a styled textarea that accepts the usual input bindings), `EmptyState` (`title` plus a default slot), and `Sheet` (`title`, with slots for the body and `footer`). Compose table rows from header/data cells and put them inside `Table`. These are native-rendered layout components; table roles are metadata and do not create a screen-reader accessibility tree in GPUI 0.2.2. An app template with the same name replaces a library entry. Library styles are plain `.ts-*` class selectors, and their colors are `--ts-*` custom properties, so `:root` restyles them.\r\n- `<img src=\"assets/photo.png\" alt=\"...\">` embeds a project-relative PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP, or SVG. Each image is limited to 8 MiB; bundles are limited to 16 MiB.\r\n- `<video>`, `<audio>`, `<iframe>`, and `<canvas>` are not supported. Compilation fails with `Unsupported HTML element <video>` (and likewise for the others), on every renderer, because the HTML parser accepts only the documented subset. For video or audio playback, open the media in the system browser (`shell.openExternal`-style link) or bundle a player outside TS-GPU; building media decoding into the native (GPUI) renderer is out of scope for 1.0.\r\n- `data-shortcut=\"mod-s\"` binds a button action to a key. `mod` means Ctrl on Windows/Linux and Cmd on macOS. `ctrl`, `alt`, `shift`, and `cmd` can also be written explicitly.\r\n- Native menus use `<menu data-app-menu=\"File\">` for an application menu and `<menu id=\"edit\">` for a context menu. Put `<menuitem data-onclick=\"actionName\">` entries and `<hr>` separators inside. Menu items may contain nested menu items for submenus. Add `data-shortcut=\"mod-s\"` to an item to bind its shortcut. Attach `data-contextmenu=\"edit\"` to an element to show that context menu on right-click. Context menus support disabled entries and static checkmarks with `checked`; checked state does not change automatically. Native application menus cannot currently disable or check items.\r\n- `data-ondrop=\"actionName\"` on a rendered container or button dispatches a `drop` event with `files`, an array of `{name, path}` objects for the files the user dropped. The host reports paths only and does not read file contents. File drops are native-renderer behavior; browser preview support may differ by platform.\r\n- `data-confirm=\"deleteItem\"` shows a native confirmation prompt, then runs the action only when confirmed.\r\n- `data-clipboard-copy=\"stateKey\"` copies a state value from a button. `data-clipboard-paste=\"stateKey\"` reads clipboard text into that state value after a button click.\r\n- `data-file-open=\"pathKey\"` opens the native file chooser and stores the selected path. Add `data-file-read=\"textKey\"` to read the chosen UTF-8 text file into another state key. `data-file-save=\"textKey\"` opens a native save dialog and writes that state's text. File reads and writes are limited to 1 MiB and require a user-selected path.\r\n- `data-fetch=\"https://api.example.com/path\" data-fetch-into=\"result\"` performs a user-initiated HTTPS GET and stores its text response. Add `data-fetch-method=\"POST\"` to send a request; the body is the state key named by `data-fetch-body`. Strings are sent as text, and non-string values are serialized as JSON when `data-fetch-content-type=\"application/json\"` is set. `data-fetch-status-into=\"status\"` stores the numeric HTTP status, and `data-fetch-error-into=\"error\"` stores an empty string on success or the error message on failure. The exact host must be listed in `permissions.network` in `tsgpu.config.json`. Redirects are disabled, requests and bodies time out after 15 seconds, and response bodies are limited to 1 MiB.\r\n\r\nExample network permission:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"permissions\": {\r\n    \"network\": [\"api.example.com\"]\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe permission schema also reserves explicit grants for the native bridge:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"permissions\": {\r\n    \"filesystem\": { \"read\": [\"./data\"], \"write\": [\"./data\"] },\r\n    \"processes\": [\"git\"],\r\n    \"notifications\": true,\r\n    \"ipc\": true,\r\n    \"window\": true\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThese grants are serialized into the app bundle. Filesystem entries are directory roots relative to the app's folder (the executable's folder, or the `.tgp` file's folder for `--bundle` runs). Process entries are executable names. Notifications, IPC, and native window APIs are opt-in booleans. Existing picker-based file operations, window title-bar controls, and network permissions continue to use their current host paths.\r\n\r\n`api.native` is the typed host API, and every call returns a promise. Host work runs on background threads, so a slow call does not freeze the window, and the action resumes when the result arrives. While an action waits, other actions and state writes queue behind it, so each one starts from committed state and rolls back cleanly.\r\n\r\n- `filesystem` (`readText`, `writeText`, `readBytes`, `writeBytes`, `list`, `stat`, `mkdir`, `remove`, `rename`): paths are relative to a granted root. `..`, absolute paths and symlinks that lead outside the root are refused. Reads need a `read` root, and changes need a `write` root. Writable roots are created at startup. `''` or `.` names the root itself, for `list` and `stat` only. Each call moves at most 1 MiB. Pass `{ root, path }` to address a root by name: a `permissions.filesystem` entry, `userData`, or a file the user picked with `dialog.openFile` (read) or `dialog.saveFile` (write), which covers that one file. `rename` takes two locations in the same root.\r\n- `process.spawn(command, args)`: runs only listed executables, without a shell, in the app's folder, with a 10 second limit and 1 MiB of output per stream. On Windows, `npm` resolves to `npm.cmd` through `PATHEXT`. Batch files take only the arguments Rust can quote safely.\r\n- `window`: `setTitle`, `close`, `minimize`, `maximize` and `restore`, each needing `permissions.window`. The window changes on the UI thread.\r\n- `notifications.show({ title, body })`: needs `permissions.notifications`. It shows a desktop toast, or a notification on macOS and Linux. On the removed WebView renderer the host showed an OS toast via notify-rust (WinRT on Windows, Notification Center on macOS, D-Bus on Linux); if the OS toast was unavailable, the call errored and `Notification.show()` in the Electron shim logged the failure, recorded a compatibility warning, and showed an in-page toast. That path is historical.\r\n- `ipc`: needs `permissions.ipc`. `ipc.send(channel, payload)` reaches the app's own `ipc.on(channel, listener)` listeners and every connected peer. Peers are other local processes. The host listens on `127.0.0.1` on a free port and requires a random token. A child process started by `process.spawn` gets `TSGPU_IPC_ADDR` and `TSGPU_IPC_TOKEN`. The peer connects, sends `{\"token\":\"<token>\"}` as its first line, then sends and receives newline-delimited `{\"channel\":\"name\",\"payload\":...}` JSON. Messages from peers reach the app's listeners, not other peers. Each backend load gets a new port, so reloading the app disconnects peers.\r\n- `app.info()` returns the app's name, version, platform, folders (`home`, `userData`, `documents`, `downloads`, `temp`, `appFolder`) and the roots it may use. `app.quit()` ends the app.\r\n- `clipboard.readText()` and `clipboard.writeText(text)` use the system clipboard (Windows only).\r\n- `dialog.openFile({ multiple, filters })` returns `{ canceled, paths }`, `dialog.saveFile({ defaultPath, filters })` returns `{ canceled, path }`, and `dialog.message({ type, title, message, buttons })` returns `{ response }`. The dialogs run on a worker thread and use the native system dialogs (Windows only). Like the removed WebView renderer, they need no grant. Picked files are granted as described under `filesystem`.\r\n- `shell.openExternal(url)` opens an `https` or `mailto` link in the default handler.\r\n- `net.request({ url, method, headers, body })` returns `{ status, headers, body }`. It needs the URL's host listed in `permissions.network` in `tsgpu.config.json` (exact host names only), redirects are not followed, and each body is limited to 1 MiB.\r\n- `power.setBlockers(types)` keeps the system or display awake while the array of `prevent-app-suspension` or `prevent-display-sleep` is set (Windows only). Pass an empty array to clear it.\r\n- `screen.displays()` returns each display's `x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `scaleFactor` and `primary`. GPUI does not report a scale factor per display, so every display reports the app window's scale factor.\r\n\r\nThe native renderer has one window and no page. `window.create`, `window.load` and `ipc.invoke` from the backend are not available; the removed WebView renderer was the multi-window path, and multi-window is on the Rendrix roadmap (M3). Messages sent with `webContents.send` are dropped, and close listeners cannot cancel a close. Each of these records a compatibility warning instead of failing.\r\n\r\nIn the browser preview (`tsgpu preview`), file and process calls run through the preview server with the same grants and path rules. The page's token and the `Host` header are checked, so other websites cannot make these calls. `window.setTitle` and `window.close` work in the page. `minimize`, `maximize` and `restore` need the native host. IPC works only within the page, with no peers. Batch files do not run in the preview, because Node cannot start them without a shell.\r\n\r\nARIA and role attributes are accepted as metadata, and `aria-label` is shown as a visible fallback for unlabeled buttons and checkboxes. GPUI 0.2.2 does not expose an accessibility tree API, so these attributes are not yet announced as native screen-reader semantics. The host keeps the parsed metadata in `native/src/accessibility.rs` (with a TODO module for the future GPUI mapping). Historical: the removed WebView renderer used the platform browser's accessibility behavior. Native screen-reader support is planned for the Rendrix engine via accesskit (Rendrix M3).\r\n\r\nTags must be properly nested and explicitly closed, except void tags such as `input` and `img`, and self-closed `<use .../>`. The document must have one root element.\r\n\r\n## Window\r\n\r\n`tsgpu.config.json` accepts `\"window\": { \"width\": 900, \"height\": 600, \"titlebar\": \"system\", \"backdrop\": \"opaque\" }`. `titlebar` is `system` (the OS title bar) or `unified` (the app draws its own). `backdrop` is `opaque` or `blur`.\r\n\r\nWith `\"titlebar\": \"unified\"`, mark the bar's drag area with `data-window-drag`. That element must not contain buttons, inputs, or other interactive elements, because Windows treats its whole area as the title bar, and clicks inside it would never reach the control. Put the window buttons beside the drag area, with `data-window-control=\"close\"`, `\"minimize\"`, or `\"maximize\"`. A unified bar without a drag area warns that the window cannot be moved. Window buttons under the system title bar warn that they have no effect.\r\n\r\n`\"backdrop\": \"blur\"` asks the window for a blurred backdrop. Windows applies it only on some systems, and the root background must be translucent for it to show, for example `body { background: #f5f5f799; }`.\r\n\r\n## Persistence and the development overlay\r\n\r\nSet `\"persist\": true` in `tsgpu.config.json` to keep backend state across app restarts. The host writes state when it changes, at most as JSON of 256 KiB, to the per-user configuration directory (`%APPDATA%\\tsgpu\\<name>\\state.json` on Windows, `~/Library/Application Support/tsgpu/<name>/state.json` on macOS, and `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tsgpu/<name>/state.json` or `~/.config/tsgpu/<name>/state.json` on Linux). On startup, values the user changed are merged into the current defaults the same way a hot reload merges them; new state keys appear with their defaults, and keys the app no longer declares are dropped. Invalid or oversized files are ignored.\r\n\r\nThe development bundle includes compile diagnostics. Press `F12` or `Ctrl+Shift+I` in the native renderer to open the development panel. The **Problems** tab lists build warnings and any failed hot-reload error, while the last successful build stays runnable. The **Inspector** tab turns the pointer into a selector: click any element to see its tag, id, classes, attributes, computed style, and state styles. Clicking controls selects them instead of running them. `Escape` closes the popup, then the panel. `tsgpu build` does not embed development diagnostics, but the panel and inspector remain available.\r\n\r\n## CSS\r\n\r\nSelectors: element, `.class`, `#id`, `*`, compounds such as `button.primary`, comma lists, descendant (`.card h1`) and child (`.card > p`) combinators. Specificity, `!important`, and source order are respected.\r\n\r\nState pseudo-classes: `:hover`, `:active`, `:focus`, and `:disabled`, on the last compound of a selector (`button.nav:hover`). They can change only `background`, `color`, `border-color`, `border-width`, `border-style`, `box-shadow`, `opacity`, and `cursor`. They do not work inside `@media`.\r\n\r\nCSS variables: declare `--name: value` (in `:root` or any rule) and use `var(--name)` or `var(--name, fallback)`. Variables resolve at compile time, and a later declaration of the same name wins. The component library's colors are `--ts-*` variables, so `:root { --ts-accent: #ff375f; }` restyles every library component.\r\n\r\nUnits: `px`, `rem`, and `em` (both 16px), `%` for sizes, insets, and padding and margin, and `vw`, `vh`, `vmin`, and `vmax` relative to the window. `auto` works for `width`, `height`, `margin`, `flex-basis`, and insets.\r\n\r\nIn the native renderer, declarations GPUI cannot render are skipped with a warning that names the file, line, and property, and the rest of the rule still applies. Set `\"strictCss\": true` in `tsgpu.config.json` to turn those warnings into build errors. (Historical: the removed WebView renderer sent the stylesheet to the platform browser engine instead.)\r\n\r\n| Property | Values |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `display` | `flex`, `block`, `grid` |\r\n| `flex-direction` | `row`, `column` |\r\n| `flex`, `flex-grow`, `flex-shrink`, `flex-basis` | numbers, and lengths or `%` for the basis; `flex: 1`, `flex: none`, and `flex: auto` |\r\n| `align-items` | `center`, `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `stretch` |\r\n| `justify-content` | `center`, `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `space-between`, `space-around` |\r\n| `gap`, `row-gap`, `column-gap`, `border-radius`, `font-size` | nonnegative lengths |\r\n| `padding`, `padding-top`, `padding-right`, `padding-bottom`, `padding-left` | nonnegative lengths or `%`, one to four values |\r\n| `margin`, `margin-top`, `margin-right`, `margin-bottom`, `margin-left` | lengths, `%`, or `auto`, one to four values, may be negative |\r\n| `width`, `height`, `min-width`, `min-height`, `max-width`, `max-height` | lengths or `%`; `auto` and `none` where they apply |\r\n| `position` | `relative`, `absolute` (`static` is treated as `relative`) |\r\n| `z-index` | an integer or `auto`, used as paint order for absolutely positioned siblings; it does not create a stacking context |\r\n| `top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left`, `inset` | lengths or `%` |\r\n| `grid-template-columns`, `grid-template-rows` | up to 12 space-separated `fr`, `px`, or `auto` tracks |\r\n| `overflow` | `visible`, `hidden`, `scroll`, `auto` |\r\n| `opacity` | `0` through `1` |\r\n| `background`, `background-color` | a color, or `linear-gradient(<deg>, <color>, <color>)` (exactly two stops; `to top/right/bottom/left` also work) |\r\n| `color` | `#RGB`, `#RGBA`, `#RRGGBB`, `#RRGGBBAA`, `rgb()`, `rgba()`, `transparent`, `white`, `black` |\r\n| `border`, `border-width`, `border-style`, `border-color` | solid borders with a width and a color; `none` removes the border. `dashed`, `dotted`, and the other styles are not supported |\r\n| `border-top`, `border-right`, `border-bottom`, `border-left` and their `-width` and `-style` properties | solid per-edge borders. Per-edge colors are not supported because GPUI paints one border color per element |\r\n| `box-shadow` | comma-separated `x y [blur] [spread] color` entries. `inset` is not supported |\r\n| `text-align` | `left`, `center`, `right` (`start` and `end` map to left and right) |\r\n| `line-height` | a length, or a unitless multiplier of `font-size` |\r\n| `font-style` | `normal`, `italic` (`oblique` maps to italic) |\r\n| `white-space` | `normal`, `nowrap` |\r\n| `text-overflow` | `clip`, `ellipsis` (GPUI draws the ellipsis when the text is clipped) |\r\n| `cursor` | `default`, `pointer`, `text`, `not-allowed`, `grab`, `move`, `crosshair` |\r\n| `font-weight` | `normal`, `bold`, `400`, `500`, `600`, `700` |\r\n| `font-family` | one family name (the first in a list; quotes are optional) |\r\n\r\nAccepted with no effect: `box-sizing`, because boxes already size the way `border-box` does. Known but not supported yet (reported as warnings): `transition`, `animation`, `transform`, `filter`, `letter-spacing`, and others. GPUI 0.2.2 has no element transform, transition, or animation primitive; the native renderer cannot emulate them without a custom animation loop and a different drawing model. Motion is therefore not available in any in-tree engine: the removed WebView renderer was the only path that supported CSS motion. The Rendrix roadmap (M3) plans rAF-driven interpolation of opacity, color and position.\r\n\r\nCustom properties with the same name in different places are global: the last declaration in source order wins.\r\n\r\nCustom fonts are bundled with the app. Add entries to `fonts` in `tsgpu.config.json`, for example `{\"family\":\"Inter\",\"src\":\"assets/Inter-Regular.ttf\"}`, then use `font-family: Inter` in CSS. TTF and OTF files up to 8 MiB each are supported. The CSS family must match the configured family name.\r\n\r\n`@media` supports `min-width`, `max-width`, `min-height`, and `max-height` in pixels. Rules are reevaluated on window resize. Grid templates with fixed, automatic, or unequal `fr` columns are laid out as flex rows with one cell per track, so both `240px 1fr` and `1fr 2fr` keep their requested proportions. Equal-weight `fr` templates use GPUI's grid. Explicit `grid-column` and `grid-row` placement is not supported.\r\n\r\n## Browser preview\r\n\r\n`tsgpu preview --project PATH` serves the same bundle in a browser at `http://127.0.0.1:4173/` (`--port` and `--no-open` are optional). It rebuilds on save and reloads the page, and the app's state carries over, merged as the native host merges it. `tsgpu preview --out FILE` writes one self-contained HTML file with no live reload.\r\n\r\nThe preview is a reference for the shared subset, not an exact match. Its layout follows the host's defaults (column boxes, border-box sizing, no button chrome), but text metrics, flex edge cases, and grid tracks differ. A `data-virtual` list builds every row, `data-window-*` attributes do nothing, file pickers use the browser's own dialogs, and `data-fetch` depends on the browser allowing the request. The backend runs in the page with the host's API and rollback rules, and with the browser's network and storage globals hidden. The page's content security policy permits script from the page itself, which the preview needs, and network requests only to the hosts in `permissions.network`.\r\n\r\n## Backend TypeScript\r\n\r\nThe configured backend entry exports a default `{ state, actions }` object. It can import and re-export relative project-local `.ts` modules (including `index.ts`); extensionless imports and `.js` specifiers resolving to `.ts` are supported. These modules are bundled at build time and watched during development. Type-only imports may refer to packages. Runtime package imports, dynamic imports, and `require` are unsupported. Node/Bun APIs and browser globals are not available in native mode. Run `tsc --noEmit` separately for type checking; bundling transpiles TypeScript but does not type-check it.\r\n\r\nActions receive typed `get`, `set`, and `update` methods plus a read-only event. `AppDefinition<State, ActionNames>` checks state and optionally the required action names. Host operations return promises and can reject when a grant is missing or an operation fails; await them and catch errors in the action. See `examples/notes` for file reads, writes, listing, and error handling. Native host operations run asynchronously; arbitrary external JavaScript APIs are not provided. (Historical: WebView mode executed the backend in the browser page under that engine's security model.)\r\n\r\nThe native backend has no general `fetch` API. Network access is limited to the declarative `data-fetch` control with an exact host allowlist. File access is available through `api.native.filesystem` inside the granted roots, and through native picker events. (Historical: WebView pages used browser APIs and browser network rules.)\r\n",
  "docs/BUILDING.md": "# Building, targets and Electron import\r\n\r\nThis page covers how a TS-GPU project is run and built: the default `run` command, builds for one or more platform targets, the prebuilt hosts those builds need, Windows packaging, and converting an Electron project. For every flag, see the [CLI reference](../packages/cli/README.md). For what each command trusts and what it can run, see [SAFETY.md](SAFETY.md).\r\n\r\n## Run a project\r\n\r\n```sh\r\ntsgpu            # same as tsgpu run\r\ntsgpu run --project path/to/app\r\n```\r\n\r\n`run` builds the project and starts it in the host for this machine. If no verified host is installed, it downloads one first, unless downloads are disabled with `TSGPU_NO_DOWNLOAD=1`. Unlike `dev`, `run` does not rebuild on save.\r\n\r\n`run` also accepts an Electron project directory. It converts the project first; see [Electron projects](#electron-projects).\r\n\r\n## Build for a target\r\n\r\nA target is an operating system and CPU pair. TS-GPU supports six:\r\n\r\n| Target | Output on this machine's build |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `win32-x64` (alias `windows-x64`) | `.exe` |\r\n| `win32-arm64` (alias `windows-arm64`) | `.exe` |\r\n| `darwin-x64` (alias `macos-x64`) | `.app` directory bundle |\r\n| `darwin-arm64` (alias `macos-arm64`) | `.app` directory bundle |\r\n| `linux-x64` | ELF executable |\r\n| `linux-arm64` | ELF executable |\r\n\r\n```sh\r\ntsgpu build                                   # this machine's target\r\ntsgpu build --target linux-x64                # one target\r\ntsgpu build --target linux-x64,win32-arm64    # a comma-separated list\r\ntsgpu all                                     # every supported target\r\ntsgpu linux-arm64                             # shortcut for build --target\r\n```\r\n\r\n`win32` and `darwin` also work in place of `windows` and `macos`. When you name a target explicitly, the output file includes it, for example `dist/my-app-linux-x64`. Without `--target`, the output name has no suffix.\r\n\r\nSeveral targets build one after another. A failure on one target is reported, the rest still build, and the command ends with a non-zero exit code. The final line says how many targets built.\r\n\r\n### Hosts for each target\r\n\r\nEach target needs a host: the prebuilt program that the bundle is appended to. A host for another target is checked against `hosts.json` from the release, then copied into the cache under `~/.tsgpu/host/` (or `TSGPU_HOME`). Host entries reference `rendrix-host-<target>` (and `tsgpu-gpui-host-<target>` while the GPUI host stays deprecated). The build copies and appends the bundle; it never runs the host for another target.\r\n\r\nIf downloads are disabled, install a host yourself, after checking its SHA-256 through a trusted channel:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\ntsgpu host install --file path/to/rendrix-host --sha256 <64-digit hash> --target linux-x64 --renderer rendrix\r\n```\r\n\r\n`--renderer native` installs the deprecated GPUI host (accepted with a warning until the Rendrix M1 gate removes it). `--renderer webview` is rejected: the WebView host was removed. Use `tsgpu doctor` to see which hosts are installed and what each one needs.\r\n\r\n## Package for Windows\r\n\r\n```sh\r\ntsgpu package                                  # dist/<name>.exe and dist/<name>.manifest.json\r\ntsgpu package --installer nsis                 # also dist/<name>-setup.exe\r\ntsgpu package --sign                           # sign each artifact with TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND\r\ntsgpu package --installer nsis --sign          # signed installer (TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND) + manifest\r\ntsgpu package --installer nsis --sign --publish-url https://example.com/app  # manual-publish reminder\r\n```\r\n\r\n`package` works on Windows only. It writes a SHA-256 manifest beside the artifacts. If you pass `--manifest-key` (or set `TSGPU_MANIFEST_KEY`), the manifest also gets an Ed25519 signature. The NSIS installer needs `makensis` and the inetc plugin. Signing runs the command in `TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND` on each file; the release guide covers the details in [RELEASING.md](RELEASING.md).\r\n\r\nElectron-builder counts differ here: `publish` has no TS-GPU equivalent. After `tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign`, upload the `.exe`, `-setup.exe` and `.manifest.json` (plus `.manifest.json.sig` when signed) to the old `publishUrl` by hand; `tsgpu package --publish-url <URL>` (or `packaging.publishUrl` in `tsgpu.config.json`) only prints that reminder, it does not upload. Electron `autoUpdater` is `unsupported` in the [coverage ledger](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/coverage.mjs) (\"TS-GPU updates are shipped by its release process, not by the app\"): remove in-app update checks and ship updates through the TS-GPU release process instead.\r\n\r\nFor macOS, `build` produces an unsigned `.app`. Sign and notarize it separately.\r\n\r\n## Electron projects\r\n\r\n### Easy transfer in 3 steps\r\n\r\n1. **Import:** `tsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app --out ./ported` (adds `-tsgpu` suffix by default; use `--build` to run the app's build first, `--force` to reuse an existing output directory, or `--fix` to preview fixes from the report without overwriting). The importer also emits a `packaging` section in `tsgpu.config.json` (`installer`/`signCmd`/`publishUrl`) for projects with electron-builder config.\r\n2. **Review the report:** open `./ported/TSGPU_IMPORT_REPORT.md`. Work the checklist top-down — each item now prints a concrete fix command, for example `add \"bundleId\": \"com.example...\"` (from `pkg.build.appId`), `set \"outDir\" to \"release\"` (from `pkg.build.directories.output`), `tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign` with `TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND`/ `TSGPU_MANIFEST_KEY` (from `win.certificateFile`/`certificateSubjectName`/`publish`), a `publishUrl` manual-upload step, and an `autoUpdater` status line, plus `Fix:` lines for permissions and `tsgpu import electron <dir> --out <dir> --fix` to preview the next fixes. Apply edits to `./ported/tsgpu.config.json` (see [Signing, publishing and auto-update](#signing-publishing-and-auto-update)).\r\n3. **Build:** `tsgpu build --project ./ported` (or `cd ./ported && tsgpu build`). If the build complains, re-check the report's `Fix:` lines and re-run with `--fix --force` to regenerate a fresh conversion. On Windows, finish the release with `tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign --project ./ported` (see [Signing, publishing and auto-update](#signing-publishing-and-auto-update)).\r\n\r\n`run`, `build` and the target shortcuts detect an Electron project: a directory with a `package.json` and no `tsgpu.config.json`. They convert it into a new temporary directory and build or run that copy. The source project is not modified.\r\n\r\nTo convert a project yourself, keep the output:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\ntsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app                # writes path/to/electron-app-tsgpu\r\ntsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app --out ./ported # chosen output directory\r\ntsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app --force        # reuse an existing output directory\r\ntsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app --build        # run the app's build script first\r\ntsgpu import electron path/to/electron-app --out ./ported --fix  # preview fixes from the report (no overwrite without --force)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe output directory must not contain the source directory or lie inside it. An existing output directory is refused unless you pass `--force`.\r\n\r\n### Signing, publishing and auto-update\r\n\r\nElectron-builder signing, publishing and in-app updates have no TS-GPU equivalent. The 3-step guide above leaves them as manual release steps; the import report and the generated `packaging` section record what to do. Use the exact commands from the report:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\n# 1. Sign with your own signtool/Azure Trusted Signing command. The path is passed\r\n#    via TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND (never committed); the command is not logged.\r\n#    win.certificateFile / certificateSubjectName map here.\r\nTSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND=\"signtool sign /fd SHA256 /a /f <your-cert.pfx> /p <password>\" \\\r\n  tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign\r\n\r\n# 2. Sign the SHA-256 manifest with an Ed25519 key (TSGPU_MANIFEST_KEY is another option).\r\nTSGPU_MANIFEST_KEY=<path-to-ed25519-key> tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign\r\n# or: tsgpu package --installer nsis --sign --manifest-key <path-to-ed25519-key>\r\n```\r\n\r\nFinal artifacts are `dist/<name>.exe`, `dist/<name>-setup.exe`, `dist/<name>.manifest.json` and, when signed, `dist/<name>.manifest.json.sig`. Publishing itself is manual: upload those files to the old electron-builder `publishUrl` (surfaced in the report as `publishUrl` and copied to `packaging.publishUrl` in `tsgpu.config.json`) and verify the `.exe`/manifest identity there. `tsgpu package --publish-url <URL>` only prints this reminder; it does not upload.\r\n\r\n`autoUpdater` is `unsupported` in the [coverage ledger](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/coverage.mjs): \"TS-GPU updates are shipped by its release process, not by the app\". Remove `autoUpdater`/`update-electron-app` checks and any `checkForUpdates`/`quitAndInstall` flow from the converted backend, and ship newer versions through the TS-GPU release process described in [RELEASING.md](RELEASING.md). Keep certificates, passwords and `TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND`/`TSGPU_MANIFEST_KEY` values outside the repository.\r\n\r\n### What the conversion does\r\n\r\n- Main-process code is rewritten to TypeScript that uses TS-GPU's compatibility runtime. Each `require('electron')` or `import` from `electron` is replaced with a shim.\r\n- Renderer HTML, CSS and scripts are copied; the converted project targets the TS-GPU renderer with the Electron-compatibility runtime. Paths that only the removed WebView renderer provided (extra windows, in-page browser behavior) no longer exist; the report lists what the app used that needs review.\r\n- Preload scripts, when they can be matched to a window, are bundled into the page. Preloads that cannot be matched are listed for manual migration.\r\n- The importer derives `permissions` from what the code uses: network hosts from `fetch`, `axios` and `https` calls, processes from `child_process`, and filesystem and window use. Review each grant. The importer does not decide whether a grant is safe.\r\n\r\n### Coverage and the report\r\n\r\nEvery Electron API the app uses is looked up in the coverage ledger ([`coverage.mjs`](../packages/cli/src/electron-compat/coverage.mjs)). Each API has one of three statuses:\r\n\r\n| Status | Meaning |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `implemented` | The shim behaves like Electron for normal use. |\r\n| `partial` | The shim runs, but part of the behaviour is missing. The report says what. |\r\n| `unsupported` | The shim exists but throws a clear error when used. |\r\n\r\nThe importer writes `TSGPU_IMPORT_REPORT.md` into the converted directory and prints its path. The report lists:\r\n\r\n- every partial, unsupported or unknown Electron API the app uses, with its file and line;\r\n- rewritten module specifiers;\r\n- the permissions it derived;\r\n- manual steps, such as dynamic paths, preloads it could not match, and Electron Forge or electron-builder settings it does not read.\r\n\r\nRead the report before you rely on a converted app. A conversion with warnings is not a finished port.\r\n\r\n### Build scripts run project code\r\n\r\nImporting runs the app's own build script in two cases:\r\n\r\n- with `--build`, always;\r\n- when `run` or `build` finds no built renderer, the importer runs `build` and tries again.\r\n\r\nThe script is `bun run build` when the project has a `bun.lock`, and `npm run build` otherwise. It runs with your user's rights. Convert only projects you trust.\r\n\r\n### Commands that do not convert\r\n\r\n`dev`, `compile` and `preview` read TS-GPU projects only. To work on a converted app with them, convert it first with `tsgpu import electron`, then run those commands in the output directory.\r\n\r\n## Troubleshooting\r\n\r\n| Message | What to do |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `No verified <renderer> host is installed` | Run `tsgpu host fetch`, or install one with `tsgpu host install`. |\r\n| `No <renderer> host for <target> is installed, and downloads are disabled` | Install a host for that target with `tsgpu host install --target <target>`. |\r\n| `Output already exists` | Choose another `--out`, or pass `--force` to reuse the directory. |\r\n| `Build script failed` | Fix the app's own build first. Run its `build` script directly to see the error. |\r\n| `Windows .exe requires a PE (MZ) host` | The host for the target does not match. Check `--target` and the installed host. |\r\n| `Unsupported target` | Use one of the six targets above, or their `windows-` and `macos-` aliases. |\r\n",
  "docs/SAFETY.md": "# Safety and trust model\r\n\r\n- This project never edits existing application installations or executables; packaging **copies** a host to an output directory before appending the bundle.\r\n- No cleanup tools, disk wipes, uninstallers, system registry edits, elevation requests, or background services are included.\r\n- The development command watches only project files and restarts only the host process it spawned.\r\n- The native binary must be trusted: use a local source build or a checksum-verified CI artifact.\r\n- Importing an Electron project runs project code. `tsgpu import electron --build` runs the source project's `build` script, and `tsgpu run` and `tsgpu build` run it when no built renderer exists. The script runs with your user's rights. Conversion writes to a new directory and leaves the source unchanged, but it does not sandbox the script. Import only Electron projects you trust.\r\n- Cross-target packaging copies a verified prebuilt host for another platform and appends the bundle. It does not run that host. `tsgpu package --sign` runs the command in `TSGPU_SIGN_COMMAND` on each artifact; see the [release guide](RELEASING.md).\r\n- The QuickJS backend has no ambient filesystem, process or network APIs. Its only host access is `api.native`, and the host checks each call against the bundle's `permissions`: filesystem paths stay inside granted roots (symlinks included), only listed executables run, without a shell, and window, notification and IPC calls need their own grants. HTTPS requests reach only hosts listed in `permissions.network`. Clipboard, dialog and external-link calls need no grant, but each dialog-picked file is granted only for that one file for the rest of the run. Host calls run on background threads. Each backend load, action, or state write has a 2-second wall-clock limit and a 32 MiB heap limit, and time spent waiting on a host call does not count against it. An action that exceeds the limit is aborted and its state is rolled back. Limits do not cover memory used outside the JS heap, so still do not execute untrusted backend code.\r\n- The Rendrix engine renders a validated document subset: the compiler rejects anything outside the documented HTML/CSS/TypeScript surface at build time, and the host draws that resolved tree directly. There is no in-page script engine and no browser surface to attack. The content-security-policy and permission model therefore applies to the QuickJS backend and the host-side grants above, not to a page.\r\n- IPC listens on `127.0.0.1` with a random token. Any process running as the same user can read the token from a child process's environment or from the app's memory, so IPC is for coordinating your own processes, not a security boundary against local malware. A peer that sends the wrong token is disconnected before any message is read.\r\n- `tsgpu preview` serves host operations to its own page only. Each request needs a random token that only the page receives, and the exact `Host` header of the preview server, so another website, or a DNS name rebound to `127.0.0.1`, cannot call them. Preview calls still run with your user's rights, limited by the same grants.\r\n- The browser preview runs the backend in a browser page, not in QuickJS, so its limits are the browser's, not the host's. The page's content security policy allows script from the page itself (the preview needs `unsafe-eval` to run the backend), and limits connections through `connect-src` to the preview origin and configured HTTPS hosts. Other request types are governed separately by the policy. The preview server listens on 127.0.0.1 only, and it serves only the page and a version number. Do not treat a preview as a sandbox for untrusted backend code.\r\n- Appending an overlay invalidates any existing signature. Use an unsigned prebuilt host, package it, then sign the finished Windows executable or macOS `.app`. macOS distribution also requires the app to be notarized for a smooth Gatekeeper experience.\r\n- The release packaging replaces the **generated output file** when rebuilt; keep backups of outputs you need to retain. It never targets existing installed apps.\r\n- Dependencies, gpui versions and GitHub Actions permissions should be audited/pinned before public releases.\r\n\r\n## Removed: WebView renderer\r\n\r\nThe WebView renderer was removed in the rewrite. The paragraphs below are its historical safety narrative, kept because the CSP/trust reasoning may matter to anyone consulting the pre-rewrite tree at `backup/webview-era`. Today the Rendrix engine renders a validated document subset (see the top of this page); there is no page, no browser engine, and no CSP surface.\r\n\r\nHistorical: WebView HTML, authored scripts and the compiled backend ran together in the browser engine and had to be trusted. Page scripts could call `window.tsgpu.invoke`; host operations were checked against the bundle's filesystem, process, notification, IPC and window grants. Those grants did not isolate scripts from each other or prevent access to browser APIs. The backend's shadowed API names were not a security boundary.\r\n\r\nHistorical: the generated page's CSP defaulted to denying resource types. Scripts from the page origin and script elements bearing a fresh build nonce were allowed; inline event-handler attributes were blocked. `unsafe-eval` remained necessary because the backend bootstrap executed its source with `new Function`; removing it required a different backend execution strategy. Inline styles remained allowed because generated and authored styles and style attributes were embedded in the page. Images, fonts and media allowed the page origin and data URLs; connections allowed the page origin and configured HTTPS network hosts; forms, frames and base URLs were restricted to the page origin, and objects were denied. The nonce authorized authored script elements, not untrusted content, and the policy was defense in depth, not a sandbox. Navigation and browser behavior outside these directives were not governed by `connect-src`. CSP also could not replace host-side permission checks.\r\n\r\nHistorical: before that policy change, `default-src 'self'` supplied a same-origin fallback; `script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'` allowed local and arbitrary inline scripts and dynamic compilation; `style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'` allowed local and embedded styles; `img-src`, `font-src`, and `media-src` allowed same-origin and data resources; `connect-src` allowed same-origin and the configured HTTPS hosts; `object-src 'none'` blocked plugins; and `base-uri 'self'` restricted base URLs. Forms and frames had no dedicated restriction. The fallback was broader than the generated bootstrap needed; the explicit resource directives remained for application content. The generated CSS and existing inline style attributes required inline styles, and the backend bootstrap required dynamic compilation.\r\n\r\nHistorical: Electron preload scripts ran in the same WebView JavaScript world as renderer scripts, before renderer scripts. A facade scoped ordinary window and globalThis property assignments to the preload, but it was not an Electron isolated world or a security boundary: shared browser objects, DOM access, indirect access to the real window, and other APIs remained available, and preloads had to be trusted. contextBridge rejected existing global names and copied and froze exposed plain objects and arrays, but exposed functions still executed in the shared world and were not a security boundary.\r\n"
};
